# Chapter 1 — Boot the Console

You turn on a fantasy console the way you turn on any computer that
respects you: it shows you a splash, it asks you where you want to go,
and then it gets out of your way. Motoi boots in under a second to a
screen with a title, a small animation, and **four doors**.

This chapter is about that first second. By the end of it you will have
opened a door, reached the prompt behind it, and gotten the console to
answer you. That last part — the console *answering* — is the whole
relationship. Everything else in this book is a longer conversation.

## The splash

When Motoi starts, the screen paints its name and, under it, four
labelled doors laid left to right:

```
                        M O T O I
                     a small console

          ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐
          │  IDE   │  │ MUSIC  │  │ ANIM.  │  │  GAME  │
          └────────┘  └────────┘  └────────┘  └────────┘
             talk       compose      tween        play
```

Each door is a **composer** — a workspace tuned for one kind of making.
But hear the important thing early, because it is the thing most
newcomers miss: **the four doors are not four programs.** They are four
front porches on the same house. The synth you play behind the Music
door is the same synth a game beeps through behind the Game door. The
sprite you tween behind the Animation door is the same sprite you push
with a button behind the Game door. There is one machine. The doors are
four good places to begin talking to it.

- **IDE** — the prompt. You type an expression, the console prints its
  value. This is where we start, because it is where the machine is
  most honest.
- **Music** — a workspace with the synth, the beat grid, and the
  transport wired up in front of you.
- **Animation** — a workspace with the tween engine and the easing
  curves in front of you.
- **Game** — a workspace with the tick loop, input, and the sprite
  table in front of you.

You can move between doors any time. Nothing you make behind one is
locked away from the others. A song you compose behind the Music door
can play inside a game you build behind the Game door, because they are
the same sound chip.

## Open the IDE door

Pick the IDE. It drops you at a prompt — a blinking cursor waiting for
you to say something. Say the most ordinary thing a computer can be
asked to say:

```motoi
(display \"hello, console\")
(newline)
```

The console prints:

```
hello, console
```

That is the entire trick of a REPL, and it is not a small trick. You
handed the machine an expression. It **read** it, **evaluated** it, and
**printed** what came back, then looped around to wait for the next
one. Read, Eval, Print, Loop — that is what REPL stands for, and it is
the loop you will live inside for the rest of this book.

## The console can do arithmetic without being asked twice

Type a sum. You do not need a `print` around it — at the prompt, the
value of whatever you type is shown to you automatically:

```motoi
(+ (* 6 7) 1)
```

```
43
```

Notice the shape. The operator comes **first**, inside the parentheses:
`(* 6 7)` is six times seven, and `(+ ... 1)` adds one to it. If that
ordering feels backwards, the Book of Scheme walks it slowly. Here we
only need one fact: **the first thing inside the parentheses is the
verb, and everything after it is what the verb acts on.** The whole
console is built out of that one sentence shape.

## Teach the console a word

The console ships knowing a lot of words. But the good part is that you
can teach it new ones with `define`, and from then on it knows them
too:

```motoi
(define (greet who)
  (string-append \"hi, \" who))
(display (greet \"Motoi\"))
(newline)
```

```
hi, Motoi
```

You defined `greet` — a word that takes a name and hands back a
greeting. Then you used it. The console did not know `greet` a second
ago; now it does, for as long as this session is running. This is the
difference between a toy and a computer: a computer's vocabulary grows
while you use it, and it grows in the direction you push it.

## The console will do a thing to every item in a list

One of the first genuinely powerful moves is asking the console to do
the same thing to a whole list at once. `map` takes a word and a list,
and hands back a new list — the word applied to each item:

```motoi
(map (lambda (n) (* n n))
     (list 1 2 3 4))
```

```
(1 4 9 16)
```

`(lambda (n) (* n n))` is a word with no name — a little function that
squares its input. `map` walked the list `1 2 3 4` and squared each
one. You did not write a loop. You described the *thing to do* and the
*things to do it to*, and the console handled the walking. Most of what
feels elegant about this console comes back to that: you say what, it
handles how.

## A countdown, because every console owes you one

Here is the tradition observed:

```motoi
(for-each display (list 'ready '- 'set '- 'go))
(newline)
```

```
ready-set-go
```

`for-each` is `map`'s cousin — it also walks a list applying a word,
but it is used when you care about the *doing* (here, displaying) rather
than collecting a result. We will lean on both constantly once the tick
loop shows up in Chapter 5.

## What you just proved

Six small programs, and every one of them ran. That is the promise this
book keeps on every page: the programs are not illustrations of code
that might work — they are code that *does* work, that you can paste
into the IDE door and watch answer. When one of them errors, the
console is telling you something true, and Appendix H is where you go to
read what.

You have booted the console, opened a door, and had a conversation with
the machine. Everything from here is that same conversation getting
longer and more interesting: next we slow down and really learn the
prompt itself — how it reads what you type, what it does with a
half-finished expression, and how to recover when you fumble one.

> **Tip**
>
> You do not have to pick a door and stay there. If you are behind the
> Game door and want to test a quick sum, the prompt is always one
> keystroke away. The console is one machine wearing four hats — swap
> hats freely.

> **Warning**
>
> Everything you `define` at the prompt lives only as long as the
> session. Close the console and `greet` is forgotten. Chapter 16 —
> *Ship It* — is how you make your work outlast the session by saving
> it as a cart. Until then, treat each session as a sketch.

